<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Askeaton</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Askeaton is a town on the River Deel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-limerick">County Limerick</a>, Ireland. Its castle, on a rock island in the river, was begun in the late 12th century and became a principal seat of the FitzGerald earls of Desmond, who also founded a Franciscan friary nearby in the 14th century. The castle was taken by English forces in 1580 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desmond-rebellions">Desmond Rebellions</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/askeaton.jpg" alt="Askeaton" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: john salter · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Askeaton,_The_Square_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1577358.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Askeaton, in Irish Eas Géitine, the waterfall of Géitine, and once also spelt Askettin, stands on the banks of the River Deel, which flows into the Shannon Estuary 3 km to the north. It lies on the N69 road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tralee">Tralee</a>, 25 km west of Limerick and 8 km north of Rathkeale, in a townland and civil parish of the same name. Desmond Castle stands at the centre of the town on a rocky island in the river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A castle has stood at Askeaton since 1199, when it was given to Hamo de Valoignes, Justiciar of Ireland. The present building dates from the time of Maurice FitzGerald, 1st Earl of Desmond, who in 1348 paid 40 shillings for the barony of Lystifti. The earls held the castle for over 200 years and ruled Munster from it, living much as Irish chiefs did: they followed the Brehon Law, spoke Munster Irish and kept poets. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerald-fitzgerald-3rd-earl-of-desmond">Gerald FitzGerald, 3rd Earl of Desmond</a>, founded the Franciscan friary in about 1389.</p><p>In the Desmond Rebellions Sir Nicholas Malby failed to take the castle in 1579, and on 9 October that year he burned the friary, killing most of the friars and wrecking the Desmond tombs. In 1580, after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrigafoyle-castle">Carrigafoyle Castle</a>, the defenders abandoned Askeaton to the English and blew up its walls. During the Cromwellian conquest the castle, held for the Confederate Catholics by Lieutenant Patrick Purcell, was destroyed in 1652 by Captain Daniel Axtell of the New Model Army, who hanged Purcell. The friars, back from 1627, left the friary for good in 1740.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Besides the castle and the friary, whose cloister keeps twelve arches on each side, the town has St Mary's Catholic church of 1851, built of local limestone after a fire destroyed its predecessor in 1847, and the redbrick remains of a Hellfire Club house of 1740 east of the castle. Askeaton railway station on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a>–Foynes line opened on 12 May 1857 and closed to passengers in 1963 and to freight in 1974. Until 1801 the town returned two members to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-house-of-commons">Irish House of Commons</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Askeaton” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Desmond earls ruled much of Munster from castles such as <b>Askeaton</b> and Newcastle West, adopting Irish language, law and patronage of poets.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-desmond">Earldom of Desmond</a></li></ul></section>
